{
  "id": "tpl-5d-upper-lower-ppl-db",
  "number": 0,
  "name": "Upper / Lower + Push / Pull / Legs · Dumbbells · 5 Days",
  "method": "flat-pyramid",
  "start_date": "2026-01-05",
  "weeks": 6,
  "origin": "pt",
  "summary": "Six weeks, five sessions needing only dumbbells, an adjustable bench and your bodyweight: heavier upper and lower days, then push, pull and legs days with higher reps. Same weight on every set. Week 6 is lighter.",
  "insights": [
    {
      "title": "Goal",
      "body": "Build muscle and strength on five days a week with dumbbells and bodyweight. The first two days use heavier compound lifts; the last three add volume with stretch-focused exercises, so every muscle gets two kinds of work each week."
    },
    {
      "title": "How it works",
      "body": "Upper and Lower are the heavier days (8-12 reps). Push (chest, shoulders, triceps), Pull (back, rear delts, biceps) and Legs use 10-20 reps. Every set of an exercise uses the same weight and reps. Paired exercises (same letter) are supersets: first exercise, about 10 seconds, second exercise, then the full rest."
    },
    {
      "title": "A days vs B days",
      "body": "Instead of A/B copies, this template uses different exercises on the heavy days (Upper, Lower) and the volume days (Push, Pull, Legs). That gives variety and trains each muscle across a wide rep range every week, which matters when the heaviest dumbbells are limited. Track each day separately."
    },
    {
      "title": "How to progress",
      "body": "When every set reaches the target reps, add the smallest dumbbell step. If your heaviest dumbbells get too easy, add 2-5 reps per set or slow the lowering to 3 seconds. Week 1 stop about 3 reps before failure, week 2 about 2, weeks 3-4 about 1-2, week 5 about 1."
    },
    {
      "title": "What to expect",
      "body": "Week 1 should feel easy while you find your weights. Weeks 2-5 should bring more reps or weight. A good week is Upper, Lower, rest, Push, Pull, Legs, rest. Week 6 is lighter: same exercises and weights, half the sets (round up), about 3 reps before failure."
    },
    {
      "title": "Key tips",
      "body": "Five days is a lot: sleep well and keep at least one full rest day. Set the bench angle before each superset. For push-ups, raise your hands on the bench if you can't reach the reps. If a movement hurts, stop it and see a qualified professional."
    }
  ],
